{
  "slug": "task-scheduler",
  "title": "Task Scheduler",
  "topic": "heap",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "pattern": "heap / Frequency math",
  "statement": "# Task Scheduler\n\nA list of tasks is given, where each entry is an uppercase letter naming the task type, alongside a cooldown `n`. Each task occupies one unit of time, and two tasks of the same type must be separated by at least `n` units containing other tasks or idle waiting.\n\nTasks may run in any order. Return the smallest total time units, including idle slots, needed to finish every task. With `n = 0` no waiting is ever required.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= tasks.length <= 50` and `0 <= n <= 20`. Each task is an uppercase letter.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "tasks = [\"A\", \"A\", \"A\", \"B\", \"B\", \"B\"], n = 2",
      "output": "8",
      "explanation": "Each type appears 3 times: frame (3-1)*(2+1)+2 = 8 slots, e.g. A B idle A B idle A B."
    },
    {
      "input": "tasks = [\"A\", \"A\", \"A\", \"B\", \"B\", \"B\"], n = 0",
      "output": "6",
      "explanation": "With no cooldown every task runs back to back, so six tasks take six units."
    },
    {
      "input": "tasks = [\"A\", \"A\", \"A\", \"A\", \"A\", \"A\", \"B\", \"C\", \"D\", \"E\", \"F\", \"G\"], n = 2",
      "output": "16",
      "explanation": "The frame needs (6-1)*3+1 = 16 slots and tasks fill them exactly: A can always be separated by the five other types plus idle-free packing across 16 units."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function leastInterval(tasks: string[], n: number)`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "The most frequent task type determines the minimum possible schedule length.",
    "Picture maxFreq - 1 blocks of width n + 1, each opened by one copy of the busiest task.",
    "Types tied at the maximum each claim one extra slot in the final partial block."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "Count each task type. The most frequent type dictates the frame: maxFreq - 1 full blocks of width n + 1, plus one slot per type tied at maxFreq. The answer is that frame size or the task count, whichever is larger.",
    "why_optimal": "Frequency counting answers in one pass instead of simulating every time unit and scanning all types per tick. On 42 generated cases PAF measured the brute force at 3.25 ms and the optimal at 0.26 ms.",
    "pitfalls": "Every type tied at the maximum frequency contributes a final slot, not just one of them. When tasks are plentiful the frame fills completely and the answer is simply the task count."
  },
  "reading_links": [
    "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greedy_algorithm"
  ],
